al-ARC grid instances; emit exactly one valid action JSON per turn and infer transformations from demos.\n---\n\n# Continual-ARC\n\n1. On every puzzle turn, including turns after feedback, output exactly one action JSON object as the entire reply, with no commentary, headings, or extra lines. Choose `submit` with a grid, `request_demos`, or `finish`; do not call tools or use shell to send the action. Match any stricter output constraint stated for the current instance.\n2. Read attempt and demo budgets. For separated panels, identify exact bounds, orientation, background, and foreground values; determine whether output preserves a panel or combines aligned cells. Compare every demo cell by cell, including unchanged cells, and retain only a rule reproducing all examples exactly. Treat observed object cells as shape evidence, not proof of how they relate to the rest of the grid: enumerate plausible roles (template, marker, or output component) and test each against the full grid and demos before committing. For noisy-looking panels, distinguish exact matches from approximate matches rather than inferring a target or transformation from resemblance alone. After an incorrect submission, treat the result only as evidence that the whole output is wrong; it gives no location information. Do not submit another guess or a small variation of it. If demo requests remain, request a demo before retrying; once received, re-evaluate the complete rule against every example and submit again only if a specific, materially revised rule is supported. If no demos remain, retry only when the supplied evidence supports a specific revised rule.\n3. Infer output dimensions and the value of every cell, not only the changed ones. When a uniform full row or column separates panels, test whether the output is one panel or combines information across panels; dimensions alone do not establish which. For any candidate cellwise overlay, test alignment and colors against all supplied examples; do no